DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ccooppyy functions are designed to copy contiguous data from one address
     space to another.

     ccooppyyssttrr() is deprecated and should be replaced with strlcpy(9).  It will
     be removed from FreeBSD 13.

     The ccooppyyiinn() and ccooppyyiinn__nnooffaauulltt() functions copy _l_e_n bytes of data from
     the user-space address _u_a_d_d_r to the kernel-space address _k_a_d_d_r.

     The ccooppyyoouutt() and ccooppyyoouutt__nnooffaauulltt() functions copy _l_e_n bytes of data from
     the kernel-space address _k_a_d_d_r to the user-space address _u_a_d_d_r.

     The ccooppyyiinn__nnooffaauulltt() and ccooppyyoouutt__nnooffaauulltt() functions require that the
     kernel-space and user-space data be accessible without incurring a page
     fault.  The source and destination addresses must be physically mapped
     for read and write access, respectively, and neither the source nor des‐
     tination addresses may be pageable.

     The ccooppyyssttrr() function copies a NUL-terminated string, at most _l_e_n bytes
     long, from kernel-space address _k_f_a_d_d_r to kernel-space address _k_d_a_d_d_r.
     The number of bytes actually copied, including the terminating NUL, is
     returned in _*_d_o_n_e (if _d_o_n_e is non-NULL).

     The ccooppyyiinnssttrr() function copies a NUL-terminated string, at most _l_e_n
     bytes long, from user-space address _u_a_d_d_r to kernel-space address _k_a_d_d_r.
     The number of bytes actually copied, including the terminating NUL, is
     returned in _*_d_o_n_e (if _d_o_n_e is non-NULL).

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
     The ccooppyy functions return 0 on success.  All but ccooppyyssttrr() return EFAULT
     if a bad address is encountered.  The ccooppyyiinn__nnooffaauulltt() and
     ccooppyyoouutt__nnooffaauulltt() functions return EFAULT if a page fault occurs.  The
     ccooppyyssttrr() and ccooppyyiinnssttrr() functions return ENAMETOOLONG if the string is
     longer than _l_e_n bytes.

